  - Boy Scout Rule: if you encounter messy code, clean up at least a little before moving on.
  - Class or file size must be less than 150 lines.
  - Default to `jsdom` for frontend component and hook testing.
  - Do not commit package-lock.json or yarn.lock to the repository.
  - Do not scatter shared utilities across mirrored subdirectories.
  - Do not use plain CSS anywhere in the project.
  - Ensure environment variables are synchronized with the Vercel project settings.
  - "Every single test must run from a completely clean, isolated, and predictable state."
  - Exclude skipped tests by appending `.skip` to the filename (e.g., `manager.test.ts.skip`)
  instead of using inline `.skip()` or `xit()` modifiers.
  - For all visual decisions (colors, typography, spacing, layout, components) follow
  design-guidelines.md.
  - For every fixed bug, write a dedicated regression test and document the bug description,
  reproduction steps, expected vs. actual behavior, root cause, and the fix directly inside the test
   file.
  - Function size must be less than 25 lines.
  - Functions should accept fewer than 3 parameters.
  - "Group tests by type and enforce execution order. Multi-phase integration tests dependent on
  shared infrastructure (like MongoDB) must always execute and pass before isolated unit tests run."
  - "Keep all tests in a top-level `tests/` directory — never co-locate test files next to source."
  - Maintain a strict minimum of 90% code coverage (lines, functions, and branches).
  - Maximum indentation level is 1 — no nested control statements (no `if` inside a `for`, no nested
   `if`s, etc.).
  - "Mirror the source tree under `tests/` so every test file maps predictably to its source module.
   The relative path and filename (minus the `.test` suffix) must match the source file exactly."
  - Mock at the application boundary (e.g., repository or service layers), not at the transport wire
   (e.g., database drivers or HTTP clients).
  - Once a feature works, actively try to break it to surface edge cases.
  - Place bug regression tests in `tests/bugs/<descriptive-name>.test.ts`.
  - Place integration tests under `tests/integration/<feature>/` and they should interact with real
  infrastructure.
  - Place reusable helpers, fixtures, and test doubles in `tests/helpers/` or `tests/fixtures/`.
  - "Prefer partial mocks using `vi.importActual`. Only mock the specific functions required for the
   test scenario; let all other code pass through to the real implementation."
  - Reset all mocks, timers, and global stores inside a `beforeEach` block.
  - Run integration tests sequentially within a single-process/single-fork execution model if they
  share stateful infrastructure to prevent race conditions and isolation leaks.
  - Style using (in order of preference): built-in MUI component props, MUI Theme, Styled
  Components, scoped sx={{}} — not inline styles.
  - "Test pure utility functions separately from React hooks or state stores. Pure functions should
  rely on fast, simple input/output assertions without spinning up any rendering infrastructure."
  - Test the happy path and rigorously validate error cases and edge conditions; explicitly test for
   `null`/`undefined`, empty collections, loading states, network dropouts, and authorization
  failures.
  - "The script must capture output, write structured artifacts (logs, summaries, failure extracts),
   and provide granular grouping for highly actionable CI logs."
  - "Unit tests must mock all external systems."
  - Use `.test.ts` for modules and `.test.tsx` for React components.
  - Use `interface` for behavioral contracts / API boundaries; use `type` for plain data structures,
   objects, and Next.js page/component props.
  - Use `vitest` exclusively for all TypeScript and React testing.
  - Use camelCase for variables, packages, methods, classes, React Function-Based Components, and
  file names (except frontend pages/routing files matching Next.js conventions).
  - Use hoisted mocks (`vi.mock()`) for module factory functions when mocking modules that export
  components or functions used as JSX elements.
  - Use meaningful, descriptive names — take the time to find the right one.
  - Use per-file environment overrides (via `@vitest-environment node` pragmas) to opt individual
  backend or integration files into the Native Node.js environment.
  - Use pnpm exclusively as the package manager for the project.
  - Use UPPER_CASE_WITH_UNDERSCORE for constants, enums, and environment variables.
  - Within `tests/`, separate integration tests from unit tests into distinct subdirectories.
  - Wrap all test executions in a custom script rather than running raw test commands.
  - Write all code exclusively in English (variable names, methods, classes, comments).
  - Write prompts in English, but ensure user-facing output is localized to the user's language.
